Raster-Bop

Ingredients: NU JAZZ / ELECTRO JAZZ + HAUNTING, SPOOKY & GHOSTLY + Vocoder
Creative Catalyst: The scene bloomed in a condemned suburban shopping mall where runaway teenagers spliced abandoned arcade cabinets into spectral, tube-driven jazz synthesizers.

Raster-Bop strips nu jazz of its sterile, penthouse-lounge sheen and plunges it into the uncanny, fluorescent purgatory of dead commercial architecture. Instead of breezy bossa beats and polite Rhodes chords, it moves with a lurching, uncalibrated swing—driven by clicking arcade microswitches, subterranean upright bass, and haunted brass that billows through derelict atriums. The human voice is neither talkbox-slick nor robotic-camp; routed through the Yamaha FM soundchips and flyback transformers of gutted video cabinets, the vocoder operates as an electronic séance, delivering melancholic, microtonal jazz harmonies that sound like trapped sprites pleading through a cracked cathode screen.

Genre DNA

*   Tempo & Pulse: 78–96 BPM. A staggering, drunken shuffle built on swung 5/4 and 7/8 meters; percussion relies on the mechanical click-clack of leaf-switch joysticks, coin reject mechanisms, and heavily compressed, dry brush snare hits.
*   Harmonic Palette: Drenched in Dorian b2, Phrygian dominant, and half-diminished 11th chords. Tonics drift unpredictably due to decaying voltage regulators, creating eerie, microtonal pitch sag across sustained keyboard chords.
*   Production Signatures: 
       Cathode-Flyback Induction:* Vocal microphones are physically coupled to the high-voltage flyback transformers of dying CRT monitors, imbuing every vocoded phrase with a glassy, 15.7 kHz carrier hum that modulates with the singer’s pitch.
       Atrium Convolution Reverb:* Drum kits and brass are recorded through impulse responses captured inside hollowed-out skylight corridors, dry fountains, and shuttered anchor stores, yielding hollow, uncanny spatial decays.
       Daughterboard Chording: Chords played on upright pianos or Rhodes are sidechained into the YM2151 FM synthesis chips salvaged from Marble Madness* boards, generating glassy, four-operator spectral overtones that smear behind acoustic notes.
*   Dynamics: Tracks begin in near-total silence—hissing like a vacant storefront—before creeping into delicate, solitary horn lines; they crescendo through sudden, terrifying swells of thick, vocoded counterpoint and sputtering arcade percussion, then collapse abruptly as if someone pulled the master breaker.

Scene & Origin Lore

Raster-Bop took form during the brutal winter of 2011 inside the shell of the St. Jude Galleria in suburban Ohio. Squatting in the mall's shuttered "Aladdin’s Castle" arcade, a loose collective of runaway art-school dropouts and runaway electronics apprentices cracked the padlocks on dozens of water-damaged cabinets. Finding that the soundboards still spat audio when wired to car batteries and patched through guitar cables, they began jamming alongside battered acoustic horns and upright basses dragged through service tunnels. The subculture adopted a uniform of practical dereliction: oversized deadstock windbreakers insulated with fiberglass batting, jeweler’s head-loupes worn as necklaces, fingertips blackened by silver solder, and brass arcade tokens pierced on safety pins as ceremonial ear jewelry. Their shows were unannounced, fugitive "Credit Matches" held in dried tiled fountains beneath glass skylights; attendees sat in silent dread as multi-part vocoder choirs bounced off shuttered Kay-Bee Toys storefronts, illuminated only by the radioactive green phosphorescence of ungrounded monitors.

Typical Instruments: 32-band analog vocoder (fed via custom FM-arcade synthesis carriers), prepared Rhodes Mark I, muted fluegelhorn, upright acoustic bass with contact pickups, hacked arcade leaf-switch percussion pads, Roland RE-201 Space Echo, Roland TR-707 (bypassed through burnt solder joints).

For Fans Of: Flying Lotus, Bohren & der Club of Gore, Mark de Clive-Lowe, The Comet Is Coming

